---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Vernacular Onboarding — Opportunity or SUS?" description="A product case study on challenging intuitively attractive solutions and discovering the real problem underneath.">
  <article class="wrap section case-study">
    <a href="/portfolio/projects" class="meta back">← Back to Projects</a>

    <!-- ── Hero ── -->
    <header class="cs-hero">
      <span class="cs-badge meta">📄 Case Study</span>
      <h1>Vernacular Onboarding — Opportunity or SUS?</h1>
      <p class="cs-lede">
        When a localization idea sounds obvious, but the real problem is comprehension, not language.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- ── Metadata sidebar ── -->
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <aside class="cs-sidebar">
        <div class="cs-meta-card">
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label meta">Year</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value">2018</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label meta">Role</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value">Product Manager</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label meta">Domain</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value">Onboarding · Localization</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label meta">Skills</span>
            <ul class="cs-skill-list">
              <li>Product Discovery</li>
              <li>Opportunity Assessment</li>
              <li>User Research</li>
              <li>Critical Thinking</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-tldr">
          <h3 class="cs-tldr-title meta">TL;DR</h3>
          <p>Challenged a popular localization feature request by reframing it as a comprehension problem. Proved that translation ≠ comprehension, leading to a simpler, more impactful redesign of the onboarding flow itself.</p>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <!-- ── Main content ── -->
      <div class="cs-content">

        <!-- Context -->
        <section class="cs-section">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">01</span>
            <h2>Context</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>
              There is a particular kind of product idea that is almost impossible to argue against. It sounds obviously useful, serves an underserved segment, and seems to have a reasonably straightforward implementation path. Vernacular onboarding was one of those ideas.
            </p>
            <p>
              The premise was simple: if a significant portion of our users were more comfortable in their native language than in English, perhaps language was creating friction during onboarding. Translate the experience into regional languages, make it more accessible, and we should see better comprehension and, eventually, better conversion.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Problem -->
        <section class="cs-section">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">02</span>
            <h2>The Problem</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>
              At first glance, it looked like a classic product opportunity. But the more I thought about it, the more I wondered whether we were jumping from an observation directly to a solution. We had evidence that language preferences existed. We had users who were more comfortable with vernacular content. But did we actually have evidence that language was the reason they were failing to complete onboarding?
            </p>
            <p>
              That distinction matters. A user's preferred language and the reason they abandon a product are not necessarily the same thing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Approach -->
        <section class="cs-section">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">03</span>
            <h2>Approach</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>
              Instead of starting with "Which languages should we support?", I started with a more uncomfortable question: <strong>"What would we need to believe for vernacular onboarding to be worth building?"</strong> That changed the problem from a feature discussion into an opportunity assessment.
            </p>
            <p>
              We started looking at the onboarding journey itself. Where were users dropping off? Were the drop-offs concentrated among users who preferred regional languages? Were users struggling to understand terminology, or were they struggling because there were simply too many things to understand at once?
            </p>
            <p>
              The deeper we looked, the more interesting the problem became. Language could absolutely be a source of friction, but it wasn't necessarily the only—or even the primary—source. Some of the onboarding experience was difficult because the product was trying to explain too much. Some terminology was unfamiliar regardless of language. Some steps required users to understand the product's mental model before they had actually experienced the value.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Key Insight -->
        <section class="cs-section">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">04</span>
            <h2>Key Insight</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <blockquote class="cs-quote">
              Translation doesn't automatically equal comprehension.
            </blockquote>
            <p>
              A badly designed onboarding experience translated into five languages is still a badly designed onboarding experience. If the user doesn't understand why you're asking for something, translating the question doesn't solve the underlying problem. If there are too many decisions, presenting those decisions in a regional language doesn't reduce cognitive load.
            </p>
            <p>
              This reframed vernacular onboarding from a localization initiative into a broader comprehension and accessibility problem. Could we simplify the flow first? Could we replace product terminology with language users naturally use? Could we progressively introduce concepts? Could examples communicate more effectively than instructions?
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Outcome -->
        <section class="cs-section">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">05</span>
            <h2>Outcome</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>
              A SUS—shiny unnecessary solution—isn't necessarily a bad idea. It can be a perfectly reasonable solution applied to the wrong problem. The skill is recognizing that before significant engineering and organizational effort gets committed.
            </p>
            <p>
              The most valuable part of this project wasn't whether we eventually decided to pursue vernacular onboarding. It was the process of challenging an intuitively attractive solution and trying to understand the problem underneath it. Product discovery isn't about validating ideas—it's about creating enough evidence to make a better decision.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Takeaway -->
        <section class="cs-section cs-section--takeaway">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">✦</span>
            <h2>Takeaway</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>
              <strong>Don't start by asking what you should build. Start by asking what must be true for building it to matter.</strong>
            </p>
            <p>Sometimes the best outcome of discovery is a new product.</p>
            <p>Sometimes it's a better definition of the problem.</p>
            <p>And occasionally, it's realizing that the feature everyone was excited about wasn't the opportunity after all.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    .back {
      display: inline-block;
      text-decoration: none;
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    /* Hero */
    .cs-hero {
      padding-bottom: 2.5rem;
      border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
      margin-bottom: 3rem;
    }
    .cs-badge {
      display: inline-block;
      padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem;
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 12%, transparent);
      color: var(--accent);
      border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent);
      margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    }
    .cs-hero h1 {
      max-width: 22ch;
    }
    .cs-lede {
      margin-top: 1rem;
      font-size: 1.25rem;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      max-width: 52ch;
      line-height: 1.5;
    }

    /* Grid layout */
    .cs-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr;
      gap: 4rem;
    }

    /* Sidebar */
    .cs-sidebar {
      position: sticky;
      top: 6rem;
      align-self: start;
    }
    .cs-meta-card {
      border-top: 2px solid var(--accent);
      padding-top: 1.2rem;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 1.2rem;
    }
    .cs-meta-label {
      display: block;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .cs-meta-value {
      font-size: 0.95rem;
    }
    .cs-skill-list {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.3rem;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
    }
    .cs-tldr {
      margin-top: 2rem;
      padding: 1.2rem;
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 4%, transparent);
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
    .cs-tldr-title {
      margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    .cs-tldr p {
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      line-height: 1.6;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      margin: 0;
    }

    /* Content sections */
    .cs-section {
      padding-bottom: 2.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
    .cs-section:last-child {
      border-bottom: none;
    }
    .cs-section-label {
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 0.8rem;
      margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
    }
    .cs-section-num {
      color: var(--accent);
      font-size: 0.8rem;
    }
    .cs-section-label h2 {
      font-size: 1.3rem;
      margin: 0;
    }
    .cs-section-body p {
      margin-top: 1rem;
      max-width: 58ch;
      line-height: 1.75;
    }
    .cs-section-body p:first-child {
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    /* Quote */
    .cs-quote {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 1.5rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      line-height: 1.4;
      color: var(--ink);
      border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
      padding-left: 1.5rem;
      margin: 0 0 1.5rem 0;
      max-width: 36ch;
    }

    /* Takeaway section */
    .cs-section--takeaway {
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 5%, transparent);
      padding: 2rem;
      border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 20%, transparent);
      border-bottom: none;
    }

    @media (max-width: 768px) {
      .cs-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
        gap: 2rem;
      }
      .cs-sidebar {
        position: static;
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
        gap: 1.5rem;
      }
    }

    @media (max-width: 480px) {
      .cs-sidebar {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
    }
  </style>
</Base>
